#!/bin/bash
#
# timeshift-on-demand-list-helper — privileged worker invoked via pkexec.
#
# Confirmed (2026-08-29 testing, this machine): `timeshift --list` refuses
# unconditionally without root — "Application needs admin access" — and
# this is unconditional Timeshift behavior (only --version/--help work
# unprivileged), not a config quirk of this machine. Real Timeshift's own
# GUI works around this by re-execing its entire process as root
# (timeshift-launcher -> pkexec timeshift-gtk); this project deliberately
# doesn't follow that pattern (see PROJECT.md, "Privilege model") — so a
# narrow, read-only, argument-free helper is the alternative: exactly one
# command, no write/delete/restore capability whatsoever.
#
# Strictly read-only. Companion's Dashboard uses this only for a glance
# (snapshot count/latest tag) — actually working with snapshots (browse,
# restore, delete) is explicitly Timeshift's job via the "Open Timeshift"
# button, never this helper. See PROJECT.md, "Snapshot management".
#
# Deliberately argument-free, same reasoning as the other two helpers.
# This is the exact program path named in
# org.timeshiftondemand.app.list's polkit exec.path annotation
# (packaging/polkit/org.timeshiftondemand.app.policy):
#
#   pkexec /usr/lib/timeshift-on-demand/timeshift-on-demand-list-helper

set -u

/usr/bin/timeshift --list
